    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "Clean build passes. The change is complete and green. Here's what I implemented to move date-derived values to a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, named for the calendar year it ends in \u2014 Australian convention matching the app's AU domain):\n\n**1. `Owner.java` (model)**\n- Added `public static int fiscalYear(LocalDate)` \u2014 the single source of truth: `month >= July ? year + 1 : year`.\n- Added the response getter `getFiscalYear()` returning `FY<YY>` (e.g. `FY27`) from the registration date, which `BuildOwner` already stamps business-day-adjusted at creation.\n- Replaced `getTenureDays()` (365-day windows) with `getTenureFiscalYears()` \u2014 elapsed fiscal years between the registration date's fiscal year and today's. The membership-points `+3` bonus now fires on tenure exceeding one elapsed fiscal year.\n- Removed the now-unused `ChronoUnit` import.\n\n**2. `AssignOwnerMembershipNumber.java`**\n- The membership number's year segment (`<customerCode>-M<YY>`) now uses `Owner.fiscalYear(...)` instead of the calendar year, so it stays consistent with `fiscalYear`.\n\n**3. `openapi.yml`**\n- Added the `fiscalYear` schema property (so the generated `OwnerDto` exposes it and MapStruct auto-maps the new getter), and updated the `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions/examples to the fiscal-year semantics.\n\nThe audit log confirms the new behaviour: an owner registered 2026-10-01 gets `membershipNumber=NSW-...-M27` and `fiscalYear=FY27` (October falls in the fiscal year ending June 2027). The acceptance test `coreReturnsFiscalYear` passes, and the full clean build is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).",
